1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
questions about the exam. Then I will take all questions about the materials for weeks one through six.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This is not your only time to ask questions. It is a time to ask questions. And then if we have no time,

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
then we will start with the week eight notes. Week eight will not be on the midterm, but time's a-wasting,

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:39,000
and this is a graduate class, so don't end early. All right, so in the last class, we were going over the

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
coordination game some more. Remember this is a classic case where you've got two strategies of left and right,

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
and we've got two equilibria of either everyone doing left or everyone doing right. So you certainly don't want

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
to have people driving on different sides of the road at the same time, because then you're going to get

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
accidents, but it's possible that everyone driving the left is worse for society than everyone driving the right,

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
for example, since most people are right-handed. Maybe it's actually safer to do it that way, so maybe the

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
American way is better than the British way, or it could be the other way around. What is clear is that it is

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
not good to combine the U.S. and British systems simultaneously on the same road, which is disastrous.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
And I've taken lots of applications, so you can think about this as a story of language. Like, why don't we

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
all have our own language? Has anyone here ever invented a new language just for fun? I said, usually

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:39,000
they do poorly. I kind of do. I've come up with a new system of simplified spelling, which has not caught on

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
at all, even though it is obviously superior to what we do, which almost anything would be obviously superior

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
to what we do in English. Let's say we do this for language, as I mentioned, so culture more generally,

17
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
what is the correct distance for having a conversation? As long as you have the same idea about how close

18
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
together you should be, then you can have conversations of comfort. If you have different views on how

19
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
close you're supposed to be, then it can be very awkward. It of course could be clothing. How come no one

20
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:19,000
here is wearing a kimono? Why not? Why is that what we wear for jobs generally instead of suits? That seems

21
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
strange. Why do we wear suits at all? Isn't that an incredibly terrible kind of clothing for people to wear

22
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
during the summer in the South? And yet that's still normal, and that's what you're supposed to do.

23
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,000
So that could be there, say. And then it's been used for technology. Since there are many technologies

24
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,000
where you want to be using the same technologies of the people, if someone came up with a totally great

25
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
new operating system, you still might not want to use it because no one else is using it, and therefore

26
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
there's no software written for it. Every now and then someone does come up with a new operating system

27
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and they try to get it going. Once in a while it takes off, but usually it falls flat. And then location.

28
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
So Paul Krugman to this day is bitter that his work on location has not been adequately appreciated.

29
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
You get to the point where you have a Nobel Prize and you sit around feeling like people adore you.

30
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
That's a problem we should all have. But anyway, the point is, look, why in the world do we need to have

31
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
the carpet production outside of Atlanta, Georgia? What is so special about that place that it needs

32
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
to happen there? And the best answer seems to be that you want to be there because all the other

33
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
carpet producers are there, and the costs are then going to be lower if you are in the same place

34
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
where you have the suppliers and the train workers and so on. But if you can move it to a different location,

35
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
if everyone can do a coordinated move, that might very well be a big improvement.

36
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Again, just think about the city of Manhattan and why in the world have it there.

37
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,000
What a stupid place to have Manhattan. Why not go and put it in a better room with a better climate,

38
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:59,000
on a place where you're not constrained by an island for your space? These seem like obvious ways

39
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
of improving things, but not too likely to happen.

40
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And again, when you talk about coordination games in the context of technology, as I mentioned,

41
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:18,000
usually we use this phrase of path dependence. There's nothing really new about this that's added on

42
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
to coordination, it's just extra vocabulary. But the idea is that the technology we use today

43
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
depends upon the technology that we're using yesterday, and the technology from yesterday

44
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
and from the day before that. And why? Because we are trying to coordinate, and the natural way

45
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
to coordinate is to just keep doing what we've been doing in the past.

46
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
You can imagine a coordinating equilibrium where we alternate every day.

47
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Where, well, since we were speaking English yesterday, let's speak French today.

48
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
That could be a rule. And occasionally you will see things like this.

49
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Like if you go to your friends, or you eat lunch with your friends a lot, a common rule would be,

50
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
well, we don't eat there today because we ate there yesterday.

51
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
So once in a while you do get this as a coordinating equilibrium.

52
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Don't do what you just did, but more normally the equilibrium is keep doing what you just did.

53
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:22,000
And then probably the most famous example of path dependence in the whole history of technology

54
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
has a name, and that name is QWERTY.

55
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
And if you're wondering what that is, just visualize typing that on a keyboard,

56
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
and you will picture your finger moving along the second row, one key at a time.

57
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Right? Anyone here use a non-standard keyboard?

58
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Once in a while?

59
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Yes, so I know German, so like...

60
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
It's got to be for typing in English, yes.

61
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
If you type in a totally different language, then...

62
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
We have the same alphabet, it's just different.

63
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Yeah, yeah, yeah, so I have typed on a German keyboard, and yes.

64
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
So yes, you can go to Germany and still type on it, but it's hard.

65
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Although now, you just press a key and then it switches it back to English.

66
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And then when you're typing...

67
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
So the Z gets moved because Z is rare in English and common in German.

68
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:21,000
So this is probably the most famous example in the whole history of technology, of path dependence.

69
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
And again, the story about QWERTY anyway is that back in the old days,

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
keyboards had physical keys, and you needed to be careful that the keys did not jam.

71
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
How many people here have ever typed on an actual physical keyboard at some point?

72
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Alright, so you've seen it.

73
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:46,000
So anyway, there's a row of keys, and if you do a key from the extreme left to the extreme right simultaneously,

74
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
they can cross and mess you up.

75
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
So the story is that the QWERTY keyboard was designed to actually prevent that.

76
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
But now, of course, have you ever had keys cross on a computer keyboard

77
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
where you press two simultaneously and the board jams?

78
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Has that ever occurred? No, that would not normally occur.

79
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
That would be a very strange thing to happen.

80
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
I mean, there might be a video game where you pretend you're on an old-fashioned keyboard

81
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and try to not cross the keys or something.

82
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
But anyway, that'd be weird.

83
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And yet, we still use the same keyboard configuration that was established 100 years earlier

84
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
when these keys were first around.

85
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
And there are stories of people claiming that there's a better keyboard out there.

86
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Which now that we don't have to worry about crossing keys would actually be far superior.

87
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Now, important to remember, there's nothing about coordination games that tells us that we tend to go to the bad equilibrium.

88
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
It just says that you could be there.

89
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
What is a game that we've seen where you do tend to go to the bad equilibrium?

90
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Prisoner of Salaam, yes, that's one where you should expect to be at the bad equilibrium.

91
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
This is not one where you should expect to be.

92
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
It just says, keep an open mind.

93
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Maybe you're in the bad equilibrium and maybe there's something better.

94
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,000
Very different.

95
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
But anyway, if someone tells you that they're there and says,

96
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
look, this is the QWERTY keyboard, this is the superior Dvorak keyboard.

97
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
So, someone may tell you that and on the one hand it's reasonable to say,

98
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
well, given that people just want to use the same thing other people are using, that seems plausible.

99
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
On the other hand, you might want to go and say, well, who are you, person who says that this is the Dvorak keyboard?

100
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Are you a Dvorak keyboard salesman, perhaps?

101
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Or might you in fact be Admiral Dvorak himself?

102
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Yes, the very man who was responsible for doing the studies claiming this keyboard was so great,

103
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,000
which were then apparently uncritically cited by people who wanted a good example of path dependence.

104
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
I'm a little puzzled by how they could not bother to check the author of the study,

105
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
so I'm not sure quite what's going on there.

106
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Maybe he did the work and then he had someone else put their name on it.

107
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,000
I don't know what the story is.

108
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
But anyway, you might want to go and double check that.

109
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,000
So, in other words, it could very well be that when someone tells you that we're here and we ought to switch to here,

110
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
that they are doing what's called special pleading,

111
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
where they are trying to claim that what's good for them personally is actually good for society in general.

112
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Like, would it all be better if we switched to Esperanto, right?

113
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Usually this is going to be said by someone who is fluent in Esperanto.

114
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:24,000
It doesn't mean they're wrong, but it seems kind of suspicious that you would be saying that.

115
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Yeah.

116
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Okay.

117
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
And then there's another possibility, like plausible for language,

118
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
that maybe if we go back in time and change things and re-run history, that would be better.

119
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
But it could still be the case that it's so costly to do the switch that it's just not worth it.

120
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:47,000
So, suppose that to go from here to here, we have to pay a trillion dollar transition cost.

121
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:55,000
In that case, you might say, wow, it's going to take longer to recover that than the total cost savings,

122
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
because the human race will be extinct by then.

123
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,000
So, better off just sticking with the language that we have got.

124
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Now, this example has been suggesting, has been debunked extensively.

125
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So, there's two economists well known to GMU, Rodolfo Salibowicz,

126
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:16,000
where they said that the supposed evidence for the superiority or equality is just made up.

127
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
And it is striking.

128
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
So, a lot of these papers were done in the early 90s.

129
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
So, since then, you might think that if there were better keyboards out there, they would have happened,

130
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
because it has gotten really easy to turn any keyboard into any other keyboard.

131
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I think that actually, if you right now, in five minutes, you could go to the Internet and download new drivers

132
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and instantly convert your keyboard.

133
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
So, has anyone ever, anyone attempted to try this?

134
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Has anyone tried?

135
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,000
They sell little mats.

136
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
I used to have a little mat with a German, and I would put it on top of my keyboard.

137
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Right. Has any of you decided to do this?

138
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
So, you're doing this now?

139
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
No.

140
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
I'm not a German.

141
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Oh, okay.

142
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
When you're actually in another country, where, again,

143
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,000
what would this model predict about what you're likely to do in another country?

144
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Do that.

145
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Yes.

146
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
When in Germany, do as the Germans do,

147
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
because then it's much easier just to conform to whatever they're doing.

148
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Right.

149
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Okay.

150
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So, yes, and one of their big points in debunking was to say that the alleged scientific proof of the superiority of the Dvorak keyboard

151
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
came from the guy who invented it.

152
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
So, it's kind of funny to take that seriously.

153
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I know. That sounds reasonable.

154
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
So, does this have implications for, like, Monopoly,

155
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:29,000
that maybe society wants a certain, like, they all want Windows, so that way you can all have Windows?

156
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, great point.

157
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Yeah, actually, you're almost to, this is very close to the very next bullet point.

158
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Okay.

159
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Yes, but worth elaborating on.

160
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
All right.

161
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
So, when are these problems least likely?

162
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
So, how about if there's a small number of players?

163
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Or Monopoly.

164
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Right.

165
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
If there's a Monopoly, then you're unlikely to do that.

166
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Or it could be there's a lot of players, but there's a few focal firms.

167
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Like, there's a whole lot of Internet firms,

168
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:59,000
but if Google, Microsoft, and Amazon all get together at a press conference and say,

169
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
we are switching to some new system,

170
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
it is pretty likely that the rest of the computer world will follow the line.

171
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Okay.

172
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:13,000
So, it is worth thinking about an alternate universe where there were ten different operating systems,

173
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and imagine how many calls to regulation there would be saying that there's a cacophony of operating systems,

174
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and we need government to go and standardize things, and the market's totally failed.

175
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Right, so, you can visualize this world.

176
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:31,000
And then, let's see, how many of you guys are old enough to even remember the antitrust case against Microsoft?

177
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Barely.

178
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:37,000
All right, so, you guys are falling or paying attention to antitrust news from a young age, I guess.

179
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Or positive.

180
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Remember means you have to actually remember what was happening.

181
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
It's not like you haven't heard about it later on.

182
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,000
All right, so, I should specify what it truly means to remember something.

183
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
You have to have been there.

184
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Do I remember World War II?

185
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,000
No, I do not.

186
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,000
I don't remember World War II, although I know a lot about it, which I've read in books.

187
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,000
All right, anyway.

188
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:08,000
So, there was a case where Microsoft was under years of antitrust scrutiny for the Windows operating system, right,

189
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and a lot of complaints about how they were monopolizing things.

190
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And the striking thing about it was that if there had been a bunch of different operating systems,

191
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:23,000
then they would be suing Microsoft, but people might be complaining for totally different reasons.

192
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:24,000
All right.

193
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Now, some of the most amusing remedies that were suggested for Microsoft.

194
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
So, back then there was a big issue that Microsoft did something really bad,

195
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:40,000
which is they built a browser to compete with the pre-existing monopoly browser, which anyone remember the name?

196
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Netscape.

197
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,000
So, there was Netscape, which had a very dominant market share,

198
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and then Microsoft built into its existing Windows operating system the Internet Explorer browser to compete.

199
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Best browser.

200
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Yes. Yes.

201
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
All right.

202
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
So, Internet Explorer.

203
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:04,000
And then one of the proposed remedies that was suggested, which I think Microsoft refused,

204
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:12,000
is they said, look, we'll settle this case if you just agree to delete the icon, not the browser itself.

205
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
They wanted them to delete the icon so that in order to get actually access Internet Explorer,

206
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:23,000
you would have to go and fiddle with the preferences to recreate the icon.

207
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:32,000
So, this is the idea of a way that we go and make things better for consumers is to require people like my parents to install an icon.

208
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:39,000
So, again, it makes total sense that Netscape wanted to protect its monopoly, which, as you may have heard, it lost.

209
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
How is Internet Explorer doing these days?

210
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
Also not doing too well.

211
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:52,000
And then there is another firm that Microsoft, Microsoft itself, which is Google, right?

212
00:14:52,000 --> 00:15:00,000
They did, you know, known for the search engine, but came along and offered a free browser.

213
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,000
All right.

214
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:10,000
So, by the way, one interesting thing to consider when you're ever thinking about predation is that there's almost no economic model that I've ever heard of

215
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,000
for companies that just give away stuff for free forever.

216
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
But this is a great deal of the Internet economy.

217
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:22,000
So, in a way, if you ever wanted a clearer case of predation, you think it would be that.

218
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:30,000
As to how in the world this is continuing, the obvious story is they're getting advertising revenue and that the customers are not, you know,

219
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
the customers are just different people than you think they are.

220
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
You're not the customer.

221
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,000
The customers are the people that are going and paying Google for advertisements and things like that.

222
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
Although, once you think about it that way, you say, rather than Google having a monopoly over search engines,

223
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:50,000
it has a small share of the market for advertising.

224
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:55,000
And then it's very unclear what the problem is supposed to be.

225
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:56,000
All right.

226
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Okay.

227
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,000
So, questions?

228
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Anyone wondering about this?

229
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Anyone happy?

230
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:02,000
All right.

231
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:12,000
One of my favorite stories of coordination is when Bill Clinton had one of his people on trial for perjury or, you know, perjury or obstruction of justice.

232
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
He was an obstruction of justice.

233
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,000
There was a juror who showed up every day in a storage ranking uniform, and this was news.

234
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,000
This news was almost as big as the news about the trial itself.

235
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
So, again, because it was not, of course, Clinton on trial.

236
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:31,000
It was one of his underlings, a far underling, who went to jail for him.

237
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
All right.

238
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:33,000
Okay.

239
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So, people happy?

240
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,000
All right.

241
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
So, yes.

242
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:45,000
I mean, I understand probably you're very sad you won't be seeing me for three weeks after this, but still, we'll try to enjoy our time together.

243
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
What era of Star Trek uniform?

244
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
I believe it was the 1960s original.

245
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Oh, the original one.

246
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Yeah, that's from the original.

247
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
All right.

248
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:57,000
So, moving on.

249
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,000
All right.

250
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
So, now let's go look at a model of bargaining.

251
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
With bargaining, let's say that we've got two strategies here, hard and soft.

252
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Hard and soft.

253
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Obviously, we could have a continuum, but we can get most of the lessons just from thinking about there being just two bargaining strategies.

254
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Okay.

255
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
And then, key point, if both players bargain hard, then there's no deal.

256
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:22,000
Right?

257
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Like if both say, I want two-thirds of the surplus, then there's no deal.

258
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Right?

259
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,000
On the other hand, if one bargains hard and one bargains soft, then the one who bargains hard gets a good deal, the one who bargains soft gets a bad deal.

260
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Right?

261
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:41,000
And then, last of all, say they both bargain soft, but they reach a deal, and the sum payoffs are higher.

262
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:51,000
And why would you think that the sum payoffs from everyone being nice would be higher than from one person being aggressive and one being wimpy?

263
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:52,000
What might the intuition be?

264
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Well, can you think about people who you know and with whom you deal amicably versus not so amicably?

265
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
You choose to deal with them more so.

266
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Yeah.

267
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,000
This is not a repeated game.

268
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:16,000
So, I think about the experience of dealing with one purchase where it's amicable versus our bargains.

269
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
Getting a bad deal is better than proposing a good deal.

270
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Yeah.

271
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
That's right.

272
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Why would the sum payoffs here be better than the sum payoffs there?

273
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Yeah.

274
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,000
Is it just because the deal is more pleasant?

275
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Yeah, yeah, yes.

276
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
You don't burn up resources fighting with each other.

277
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:38,000
So, if there's a big dispute and someone makes a big deal about it, then you're suing them.

278
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
You may just burn up time, for example.

279
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
So, if everyone's soft, then it's like, oh, great.

280
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,000
All right, fine.

281
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:44,000
You were nice.

282
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Whereas if one person bargains hard and another person bargains soft, then Barbara's like, I want this, and I want this, and I want this.

283
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
And the soft bargain's like, gee, really?

284
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:52,000
You have to have all those things?

285
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:53,000
Yes.

286
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Uh-huh.

287
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Uh-huh.

288
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,000
Yes.

289
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,000
All right.

290
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
So, you burn up some resources that way.

291
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Okay.

292
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:01,000
All right.

293
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Now, notice.

294
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
So, here we've got our two-tier strategy in hash equilibrium, right?

295
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:10,000
But we also have a mixed strategy in hash equilibrium.

296
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:16,000
And, again, since I've set this up as a totally symmetric game, then a general good guess

297
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
at everything symmetric is that there's two possibilities.

298
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:22,000
It is that the mixed strategy in hash equilibrium will be?

299
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:25,000
Yeah, 1-half, 1-half, or 0.5, 0.5, 0.5.

300
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
All right, so 0.5, 0.5.

301
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:28,000
All right.

302
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
And so, this is a very interesting one.

303
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:35,000
So, one thing is that if you plug in some numbers, you notice that it's stable.

304
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
What does it mean?

305
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:44,000
Well, you'll see that if you go up to 51% hard, then there's a higher expected payoff for playing soft.

306
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:51,000
If you go down to 49% hard, then there's a higher payoff to playing hard.

307
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:52,000
All right.

308
00:19:52,000 --> 00:20:00,000
So, it's stable in that if you deviate a little bit from the equilibrium, then people want to switch to the opposite.


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
And that drives you back to this mixed strategy

2
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:07,560
and Nash equilibrium.

3
00:20:07,560 --> 00:20:10,560
A lot of people like a physical metaphor.

4
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:14,000
So suppose that we've got a bunch of mountains here,

5
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,640
and we put a ball perfectly at the top.

6
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:19,360
It's exactly balanced at the top.

7
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:21,120
Is that an equilibrium?

8
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:21,620
Yes.

9
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:22,120
Sure.

10
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:23,640
Is it a stable equilibrium?

11
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:25,720
No, because if the ball goes a little to the left,

12
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:28,040
a little bit right, you fall down to the valley.

13
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:30,360
On the other hand, suppose we've got a ball down there.

14
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:31,360
Is that an equilibrium?

15
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:31,860
Yes.

16
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:33,120
Is that a stable equilibrium?

17
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:33,620
Yes.

18
00:20:33,620 --> 00:20:35,700
Because if you happen to get blown a little bit left

19
00:20:35,700 --> 00:20:38,680
or right, you tend to go right back to where you started.

20
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:40,960
Of course, this doesn't mean that a really big gust

21
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:43,360
couldn't blow you to something totally different.

22
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:45,720
But stability is about local stability.

23
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:47,440
If you just move a little bit either way,

24
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:49,920
you tend to go back or not.

25
00:20:49,920 --> 00:20:53,120
This is a physical metaphor for equilibrium,

26
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:56,360
which is of some use.

27
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:59,000
If you're used to thinking about physical objects,

28
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,040
then that's a useful metaphor.

29
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:04,280
On the other hand, if you just don't like doing that,

30
00:21:04,280 --> 00:21:08,360
then it's just going to confuse you more.

31
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:10,360
Now, the intuition about this mixed strategy

32
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:12,840
and extra equilibrium is that in equilibrium,

33
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:15,320
both strategies are equally good.

34
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:18,320
So the hard strategy is better if you

35
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:20,960
happen to bump into a soft bargainer,

36
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:23,760
but it's worse if you happen to bump into a hard bargainer.

37
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:25,880
The soft bargaining strategy is better

38
00:21:25,880 --> 00:21:29,520
if you happen to bump into another soft bargainer.

39
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:32,200
You bump into a hard bargainer, then you've got a problem.

40
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:34,720
But still, it's better than bumping

41
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:36,600
into the hard bargainer with a hard strategy,

42
00:21:36,600 --> 00:21:39,520
where you get nothing at all.

43
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:41,440
Selenberg had a line where he says,

44
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:44,520
don't mistake a hard bargainer for a good bargainer.

45
00:21:44,520 --> 00:21:48,720
Again, in the model, hard bargainers are, in fact,

46
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:49,640
not worse.

47
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:51,200
They're just mediocre.

48
00:21:51,200 --> 00:21:52,560
It's nothing special.

49
00:21:52,560 --> 00:21:55,560
And again, sort of the Donald Trump style

50
00:21:55,560 --> 00:21:58,120
art of the deal, where there's a whole lot of advice saying,

51
00:21:58,120 --> 00:22:00,480
be a hard bargainer.

52
00:22:00,480 --> 00:22:03,520
This is not, in fact, if it were as easy as that,

53
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:05,000
then everybody would do it.

54
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:06,640
And of course, if it is, and if that's

55
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:08,760
what everybody were like, then there'd be no deal.

56
00:22:08,760 --> 00:22:10,600
So how can that be great?

57
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:12,560
You might say, well, you want everyone to think

58
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:13,920
of you as a hard bargainer.

59
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:17,920
Even that is not clear, as long as people have other options.

60
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:21,160
So if you're the only possible partner in the world,

61
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:24,640
then you might want the reputation as a hard bargainer.

62
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:27,040
Then people will just commission it to you without a fight

63
00:22:27,040 --> 00:22:28,520
because they know it's helpless.

64
00:22:28,520 --> 00:22:30,160
But if they can just say, hey, I don't

65
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:32,800
want to deal with a person that would write a book like this.

66
00:22:32,800 --> 00:22:35,800
Of course, the marketing of the book itself is contradictory.

67
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:38,520
If you really believed in the strategy of art of the deal,

68
00:22:38,520 --> 00:22:40,960
you'd be selling the book for $100,000 a copy.

69
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:43,160
And just saying, hey, this is what you have to pay for

70
00:22:43,160 --> 00:22:44,840
by great advice.

71
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:47,280
There's no better advice than this than any other book.

72
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:48,720
So if you don't want it, too bad.

73
00:22:48,720 --> 00:22:50,680
You've got to pay if you want it.

74
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:54,600
So and yet, the book, of course, is price to sell.

75
00:22:54,600 --> 00:22:55,100
Yes?

76
00:22:55,100 --> 00:22:56,800
You could build a reputation as a hard bargainer

77
00:22:56,800 --> 00:22:58,200
and just give people good deals.

78
00:22:58,200 --> 00:22:59,800
And they'll think that they got one wrong

79
00:22:59,800 --> 00:23:00,600
and they want to make the deal.

80
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:03,320
There is that possibility as well.

81
00:23:03,320 --> 00:23:05,080
Yet, of course, if they really think

82
00:23:05,080 --> 00:23:06,960
they're not going to get a good deal for you,

83
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:09,040
they won't even want to listen to your sales pitch.

84
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:11,560
So you don't want it to be too extreme.

85
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:17,160
So we're not doing any psychology yet.

86
00:23:17,160 --> 00:23:20,280
My personal view is that probably hard bargaining

87
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:21,760
is overused in general.

88
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:23,840
And we're not actually at the mixed strategy

89
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:25,080
in Nash equilibrium.

90
00:23:25,080 --> 00:23:26,880
And I've just seen too many people fighting

91
00:23:26,880 --> 00:23:28,120
for no good reason.

92
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:29,480
Where it's like, well, gee, why don't you just

93
00:23:29,480 --> 00:23:30,680
go and cooperate with people?

94
00:23:30,680 --> 00:23:31,880
Wouldn't that be easier?

95
00:23:31,880 --> 00:23:34,360
So I know a lot more cases of people

96
00:23:34,360 --> 00:23:36,920
who have ruined relationships while sticking

97
00:23:36,920 --> 00:23:40,440
to some stupid principle no one else ever heard of.

98
00:23:40,440 --> 00:23:43,400
On the theory that other people are trying to exploit them.

99
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:45,900
And it's like, look, don't they understand this is the rule?

100
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:47,480
I've never heard of this rule before.

101
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:50,600
Probably the people you're mad at haven't heard of it either.

102
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:53,360
So that's just my personal view of things

103
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:54,560
that I've got.

104
00:23:54,560 --> 00:23:56,840
We're not presenting any evidence here of this.

105
00:23:56,840 --> 00:24:00,960
But in general, part of my general view

106
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:03,160
is that we have a bunch of emotions which perhaps

107
00:24:03,160 --> 00:24:05,200
were functional when we were cavemen,

108
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:09,360
but are now highly dysfunctional, such as rage.

109
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:12,200
It's like, look, in general, if you just turn your rage down

110
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:14,600
to one on a scale from 0 to 10, you

111
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:17,000
would have a more successful life overall.

112
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:21,080
And yet most people are up at a three or a four.

113
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:24,280
How often has rage actually accomplished anything for you?

114
00:24:27,160 --> 00:24:28,040
Maybe you can come up.

115
00:24:28,040 --> 00:24:30,560
So no doubt you can come up with some example or other.

116
00:24:30,560 --> 00:24:32,960
But just think about all the times

117
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:35,000
that your rage has actually caused trouble for you

118
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:36,160
and achieved nothing.

119
00:24:36,160 --> 00:24:37,600
Or it could be counterproductive.

120
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:39,000
You've made enemies.

121
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,320
So you lost a deal and you also lost this deal

122
00:24:42,320 --> 00:24:45,880
and you also destroyed the relationship on top of it.

123
00:24:45,880 --> 00:24:48,920
But that's nothing to worry about right now.

124
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:51,600
Now, this mixed strategy Nash equilibrium is not first best.

125
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:54,080
First best would be to always be there.

126
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:55,520
That's first best.

127
00:24:55,520 --> 00:25:00,080
Always be at the outcome that maximizes the sum of payoffs.

128
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:07,000
But the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium

129
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,800
only gives you that outcome 25% of the time.

130
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:12,200
So it's not terrible.

131
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:15,280
And as that outcome gets worse and worse,

132
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:17,760
if we're talking about we both starved to death,

133
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:19,320
then the probability of that happening

134
00:25:19,320 --> 00:25:21,640
gets smaller and smaller.

135
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:23,600
And remember, since you only get there

136
00:25:23,600 --> 00:25:26,360
if both people have played the hard strategy,

137
00:25:26,360 --> 00:25:27,920
then if that outcome is really bad,

138
00:25:27,920 --> 00:25:31,760
then it's going to be extremely unlikely that would ever occur.

139
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:33,840
That's balanced by the fact that when it happens,

140
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:35,280
it's a disaster.

141
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:38,080
So keep that in mind.

142
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:41,240
But again, so people like you to think

143
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:43,240
they're going to play hard.

144
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:44,840
But again, the problem is precisely

145
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:46,840
because this perception is value,

146
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:48,880
it's very common for people to not really

147
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:52,040
be as aggressive in bargaining as they say they'll be.

148
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:54,880
So I remember a couple of times my wife told someone,

149
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:58,400
I'll pay that not a penny more.

150
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:01,880
And then generally, so she says that.

151
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:04,040
But it's her, maybe you should believe her actually.

152
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:05,960
But other people don't know that.

153
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:09,240
And so this is not really all the, so people, oh my god,

154
00:26:09,240 --> 00:26:10,700
she said wouldn't pay a penny more.

155
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:12,720
So well, then let's give her that exact deal.

156
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:14,280
Or there's no point.

157
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:18,240
I end up further and further into the discussion.

158
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:20,360
All right, now what's kind of nice about this model

159
00:26:20,360 --> 00:26:23,120
is that when you do see two people failing to reach a deal

160
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:25,000
and it's clear that it's mutually beneficial,

161
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,800
it's tempting just to say they're both stupid, which

162
00:26:27,800 --> 00:26:30,960
is a theory we should never dismiss.

163
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:32,720
You can spend a lot of time in economics,

164
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:35,640
but don't lose the basic juvenile theory

