00:58:07,040 --> 00:58:10,880
so there's a book by Cass Sunstein and co-author Stephen

454
00:58:10,880 --> 00:58:16,120
Holmes, where they say that all property comes from government.

455
00:58:16,120 --> 00:58:20,840
And when I met him, I said, how about black market property?

456
00:58:20,840 --> 00:58:23,760
That, first of all, is not recognized by the government.

457
00:58:23,760 --> 00:58:25,960
In fact, governments usually say it's illegal.

458
00:58:25,960 --> 00:58:28,240
And yet, what is a really good way

459
00:58:28,240 --> 00:58:30,640
to get a drug dealer to try to kill you?

460
00:58:30,640 --> 00:58:31,480
Take his stash.

461
00:58:31,480 --> 00:58:32,760
Take his stash.

462
00:58:32,760 --> 00:58:34,000
That's right.

463
00:58:34,000 --> 00:58:37,440
It's like, so that's interesting.

464
00:58:37,440 --> 00:58:38,960
So you were saying, look, the law

465
00:58:38,960 --> 00:58:43,240
doesn't recognize your stash, so I can take it if I want.

466
00:58:43,240 --> 00:58:45,200
Well, I recognize it.

467
00:58:45,200 --> 00:58:47,360
And now I'm going to kill you.

468
00:58:47,360 --> 00:58:49,840
This is the time for you to be saying, oh my god, oh my god,

469
00:58:49,840 --> 00:58:50,400
I'm so sorry.

470
00:58:50,400 --> 00:58:53,840
It was totally your stash, not to be having a dispute

471
00:58:53,840 --> 00:58:56,280
and to be being difficult.

472
00:58:56,280 --> 00:58:57,960
Now again, so obviously, if government

473
00:58:57,960 --> 00:58:59,720
were the sole source of property rights,

474
00:58:59,720 --> 00:59:01,300
this whole thing would not make sense.

475
00:59:01,300 --> 00:59:04,040
It would be very hard to have black markets.

476
00:59:04,040 --> 00:59:06,880
And yet, black markets have been known to thrive.

477
00:59:06,880 --> 00:59:09,320
So one of my undergraduate teachers

478
00:59:09,320 --> 00:59:10,720
was a guy named Gregory Grossman,

479
00:59:10,720 --> 00:59:14,080
who was the big expert on black markets in the Eastern Bloc.

480
00:59:14,080 --> 00:59:16,520
By his estimate, the underground economy

481
00:59:16,520 --> 00:59:18,880
added a full third to the Soviet economy.

482
00:59:22,760 --> 00:59:26,440
And so yet, this is in a state where

483
00:59:26,440 --> 00:59:28,320
private property and the means of production

484
00:59:28,320 --> 00:59:29,400
is virtually illegal.

485
00:59:29,400 --> 00:59:31,920
And yet, there's a massive economy going on.

486
00:59:31,920 --> 00:59:33,440
And how is this possible?

487
00:59:33,440 --> 00:59:36,560
It's because these notions of territory and property

488
00:59:36,560 --> 00:59:38,120
are not just legal.

489
00:59:38,120 --> 00:59:41,320
They're also very psychologically rooted.

490
00:59:41,320 --> 00:59:44,720
And because of the psychology, it creates focal points.

491
00:59:44,720 --> 00:59:49,200
And those focal points lead to changes in behavior.

492
00:59:49,200 --> 00:59:51,800
Because again, if there wasn't anything like this going on,

493
00:59:51,800 --> 00:59:53,720
then consider these two scenarios.

494
00:59:53,720 --> 00:59:57,480
One is you go and accidentally destroy $100,000

495
00:59:57,480 --> 00:59:59,120
worth of a drug dealer's cocaine.

496
00:59:59,120 --> 01:00:11,680
And then he says, you owe me $100,000.


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:05,040
You better get me, you better find me, you better pay me back.

2
01:00:05,040 --> 01:00:06,040
There's that.

3
01:00:06,040 --> 01:00:09,640
Or there could be a scenario where he just comes up to a total stranger and says, hey,

4
01:00:09,640 --> 01:00:12,240
give me $100,000 or I'll kill you.

5
01:00:12,240 --> 01:00:15,600
In terms of the incentives, those seem very similar.

6
01:00:15,600 --> 01:00:18,560
But in terms of the focal points, they're very dissimilar.

7
01:00:18,560 --> 01:00:20,560
The first one, there's a common understanding.

8
01:00:20,560 --> 01:00:26,800
Oh my God, I went and destroyed his property and so now he and I both understand that I

9
01:00:26,800 --> 01:00:27,800
owe him.

10
01:00:27,800 --> 01:00:32,040
Whereas if someone just comes and tries to extort money out of you for no reason, that's

11
01:00:32,040 --> 01:00:36,040
one where you're probably a lot more likely to go to the police.

12
01:00:36,040 --> 01:00:40,120
Or of course there you can say, well, I can go and explain the situation, but look at

13
01:00:40,120 --> 01:00:42,320
what we're just, I accidentally destroyed his cocaine.

14
01:00:42,320 --> 01:00:43,960
Look, I'm not a drug dealer.

15
01:00:43,960 --> 01:00:45,240
I've got nothing to do with this.

16
01:00:45,240 --> 01:00:48,800
I just happen to be running my hose when it hit his cocaine.

17
01:00:48,800 --> 01:00:50,560
I don't know.

18
01:00:50,560 --> 01:00:58,240
So even there, it's probably going to lead to two very different scenarios.

19
01:00:58,240 --> 01:00:59,920
Anyway, so interesting.

20
01:00:59,920 --> 01:01:05,600
And again, in terms of how socially disruptive this is, remember there's a paper on preschoolers.

21
01:01:05,600 --> 01:01:08,880
Is it normal to go to preschoolers and sit around telling them, if anyone tries to take

22
01:01:08,880 --> 01:01:09,880
your stuff, you hit them?

23
01:01:09,880 --> 01:01:14,000
Is that a normal thing we tell small children?

24
01:01:14,000 --> 01:01:18,600
What do we actually propagandize kids to do with their stuff?

25
01:01:18,600 --> 01:01:19,760
Share.

26
01:01:19,760 --> 01:01:25,600
So why do we give propaganda to tell them to do stuff that is, what is human nature?

27
01:01:25,600 --> 01:01:27,400
You don't normally do propaganda for human nature.

28
01:01:27,400 --> 01:01:28,400
Things that come naturally.

29
01:01:28,400 --> 01:01:30,400
You do propaganda for things that don't come naturally.

30
01:01:30,400 --> 01:01:34,640
Because the thing that comes naturally is having a developed sense of territory and

31
01:01:34,640 --> 01:01:35,640
property.

32
01:01:35,640 --> 01:01:39,240
The main thing you might say that little kids don't have is the social intelligence to realize

33
01:01:39,240 --> 01:01:42,360
that other people have the same feelings that they do.

34
01:01:42,360 --> 01:01:45,680
Although again, they usually learn that pretty quickly because you try to take another kid's

35
01:01:45,680 --> 01:01:51,160
stuff and then they cry or get mad or hit you.

36
01:01:51,160 --> 01:01:52,560
So questions about that?

37
01:01:52,560 --> 01:01:53,560
Everyone happy?

38
01:01:53,560 --> 01:01:57,000
How did Susskind reply to your point about...

39
01:01:57,000 --> 01:01:58,000
He immediately conceded the point.

40
01:01:58,000 --> 01:02:03,480
Then I talked to another law professor who said, yeah, but the next day he'll forget

41
01:02:03,480 --> 01:02:05,040
that he ever made the concession.

42
01:02:05,040 --> 01:02:08,040
But it was still pretty good.

43
01:02:08,040 --> 01:02:10,840
Better than what usually happens.

44
01:02:10,840 --> 01:02:13,920
Most of the law professors especially are highly disputatious.

45
01:02:13,920 --> 01:02:19,040
And so getting them to concede anything is pretty hard.

46
01:02:19,040 --> 01:02:20,600
So questions about this?

47
01:02:20,600 --> 01:02:21,760
Everybody happy?

48
01:02:21,760 --> 01:02:22,760
All right.

49
01:02:22,760 --> 01:02:23,760
Good.

50
01:02:23,760 --> 01:02:24,760
All right.

51
01:02:24,760 --> 01:02:27,160
So last topic for the exam.

52
01:02:27,160 --> 01:02:28,160
Then seeking and lobbying.

53
01:02:28,160 --> 01:02:29,160
All right.

54
01:02:29,160 --> 01:02:39,400
So remember we already went and talked about two kinds of inefficiency in markets.

55
01:02:39,400 --> 01:02:44,080
And then we first started talking about...

56
01:02:44,080 --> 01:02:53,320
First started talking about a case where say, firm two here goes and charges a price above

57
01:02:53,320 --> 01:02:58,200
its own marginal cost, which leads to this allocated inefficiency.

58
01:02:58,200 --> 01:02:59,200
All right.

59
01:02:59,200 --> 01:03:05,800
And then we talked about the second kind of inefficiency caused by a situation where it's

60
01:03:05,800 --> 01:03:11,520
illegal for the low-cost firm to produce, most obviously.

61
01:03:11,520 --> 01:03:16,680
So it's illegal for the more efficient firm to produce, in which case we also get, besides

62
01:03:16,680 --> 01:03:23,920
the triangle, we get this trapezoid of productive inefficiency.

63
01:03:23,920 --> 01:03:29,360
Now we're ready to go for a third kind of inefficiency, the least discussed of all.

64
01:03:29,360 --> 01:03:31,120
We weren't totally sure.

65
01:03:31,120 --> 01:03:35,880
So productive inefficiency has so fallen out of discussion that maybe actually the outcome

66
01:03:35,880 --> 01:03:36,880
is actually close.

67
01:03:36,880 --> 01:03:37,880
All right.

68
01:03:37,880 --> 01:03:38,880
I need to get a rubber.

69
01:03:38,880 --> 01:03:42,360
It hasn't fallen out because the estimates are small.

70
01:03:42,360 --> 01:03:47,520
It falls out because economists, like most human beings, are subject to fads.

71
01:03:47,520 --> 01:03:51,000
And they want to talk about what other people are talking about, even if what other people

72
01:03:51,000 --> 01:03:53,760
are talking about isn't very important.

73
01:03:53,760 --> 01:03:58,520
And it's a sad, empathetic commentary on academia that people are like this, even the numerate.

74
01:03:58,520 --> 01:04:03,200
It's one thing for innumerate people to get focused on things that aren't very important,

75
01:04:03,200 --> 01:04:06,840
but for supposedly numerate people to get focused on things that are important is particularly

76
01:04:06,840 --> 01:04:07,840
ridiculous.

77
01:04:07,840 --> 01:04:11,680
So of course, you have to be the change you want to see in the world and work on the topics

78
01:04:11,680 --> 01:04:12,680
that are quantitatively important.

79
01:04:12,680 --> 01:04:16,840
At the same time, in order to do that, you need to get a job.

80
01:04:16,840 --> 01:04:19,680
So it's a tough balancing act, but that's what tenure is for.

81
01:04:19,680 --> 01:04:25,400
Tenure is the time to finally become supernumerate in what problems you actually focus on.

82
01:04:25,400 --> 01:04:26,400
Anyway.

83
01:04:26,400 --> 01:04:27,400
Okay.

84
01:04:27,400 --> 01:04:30,800
So a last form of inefficiency has a bunch of different names.

85
01:04:30,800 --> 01:04:35,720
So sometimes it's called lobbying inefficiency or probably most commonly rent-seeking.

86
01:04:35,720 --> 01:04:42,320
And there's about five other names that I won't bother you with directly on productive

87
01:04:42,320 --> 01:04:43,320
activities.

88
01:04:43,320 --> 01:04:44,320
A bunch of others.

89
01:04:44,320 --> 01:04:50,160
So anyway, this stuff arises when people use resources to transfer resources.

90
01:04:50,160 --> 01:04:53,280
When you use resources to transfer resources.

91
01:04:53,280 --> 01:04:54,280
All right.

92
01:04:54,280 --> 01:04:58,640
Such as in lobbying, where when you lobby, you go and hire a team of lobbyists.

93
01:04:58,640 --> 01:05:00,400
And what are you trying to get for yourselves?

94
01:05:00,400 --> 01:05:01,400
Stuff.

95
01:05:01,400 --> 01:05:05,320
Give me stuff because I hired a team of lobbyists.

96
01:05:05,320 --> 01:05:07,760
Rent-seeking is the same idea.

97
01:05:07,760 --> 01:05:14,000
Anything, anytime that you are trying to change the distribution of resources by spending

98
01:05:14,000 --> 01:05:17,600
some resources, you are engaged in this.

99
01:05:17,600 --> 01:05:22,160
Now a great example of this would be trying to lobby for monopoly privilege.

100
01:05:22,160 --> 01:05:27,600
So suppose you are fighting to be the only company in a city allowed to sell cable television.

101
01:05:27,600 --> 01:05:28,600
All right.

102
01:05:28,600 --> 01:05:35,560
So you would go and hire your team of experts and lawyers and consultants and political

103
01:05:35,560 --> 01:05:40,040
analysts and former and friends of people in power.

104
01:05:40,040 --> 01:05:42,360
You assemble your dream team there.

105
01:05:42,360 --> 01:05:43,360
And of course you pay them.

106
01:05:43,360 --> 01:05:47,320
And their job is to get you that piece of paper saying that you're the only firm that

107
01:05:47,320 --> 01:05:48,320
can do it.

108
01:05:48,320 --> 01:05:49,320
Right.

109
01:05:49,320 --> 01:05:50,320
Of course, you're payers.

110
01:05:50,320 --> 01:05:51,320
You're likely to be doing the same thing.

111
01:05:51,320 --> 01:05:52,320
All right.

112
01:05:52,320 --> 01:05:55,080
So firms pressure the government to become the sole leader producer.

113
01:05:55,080 --> 01:05:59,000
And the more that you spend, normally of course, the better your chances of getting your way

114
01:05:59,000 --> 01:06:00,000
are.

115
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:01,000
All right.

116
01:06:01,000 --> 01:06:05,000
So the nice metaphor of this comes from the game of tug of war.

117
01:06:05,000 --> 01:06:09,000
Anyone ever play this game where there's a rope and there's mud in the center and two

118
01:06:09,000 --> 01:06:11,000
teams try to pull the other side in?

119
01:06:11,000 --> 01:06:12,000
All right.

120
01:06:12,000 --> 01:06:13,000
So if there's people here.

121
01:06:13,000 --> 01:06:14,000
There's a person one here.

122
01:06:14,000 --> 01:06:15,000
There's a person two.

123
01:06:15,000 --> 01:06:16,000
See why I'm nodding our heads.

124
01:06:16,000 --> 01:06:27,000
And then there's a rope and then there's like another person over here on the other side.

125
01:06:27,000 --> 01:06:30,000
And then there's a mud pile in there.

126
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:31,000
All right.

127
01:06:31,000 --> 01:06:34,000
And if it's two versus two people against one, this guy's probably going to lose.

128
01:06:34,000 --> 01:06:36,000
So he's going to get pulled in the mud.

129
01:06:36,000 --> 01:06:41,160
But he might go and hire a helper.

130
01:06:41,160 --> 01:06:43,160
And then maybe this side will hire a few more helpers.

131
01:06:43,160 --> 01:06:45,000
And this side will hire a few more helpers.

132
01:06:45,000 --> 01:06:46,000
All right.

133
01:06:46,000 --> 01:06:50,000
And in the end, you can wind up burning a ton of resources in this contest, which is

134
01:06:50,000 --> 01:06:51,000
like tug of war.

135
01:06:51,000 --> 01:06:52,000
All right.

136
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:56,000
Only in the real world, normally, if you win, then you get the policy you want.

137
01:06:56,000 --> 01:06:57,000
You get the favorite policy.

138
01:06:57,000 --> 01:07:02,000
On the other side, winds up having wasted all this lobbying efforts trying to influence

139
01:07:02,000 --> 01:07:04,000
policy even though the government did not listen.

140
01:07:04,000 --> 01:07:05,000
Yes.

141
01:07:05,000 --> 01:07:10,000
I remember only a week or two ago, someone was talking to me about how for all the money

142
01:07:10,000 --> 01:07:14,000
the Koch family spent on politics, it's not that clear what policy changes they've been

143
01:07:14,000 --> 01:07:19,000
able to affect, which might lead you to say that their policy is ineffective, or rather

144
01:07:19,000 --> 01:07:21,000
the lobbying is ineffective.

145
01:07:21,000 --> 01:07:24,000
Another story you could have is you should see what would have happened if we hadn't

146
01:07:24,000 --> 01:07:25,000
done it.

147
01:07:25,000 --> 01:07:27,000
So who knows what that would be.

148
01:07:27,000 --> 01:07:31,000
So there's a whole lot of people who have their hands on the rope, all trying to go

149
01:07:31,000 --> 01:07:33,000
and do it.

150
01:07:33,000 --> 01:07:34,000
OK.

151
01:07:34,000 --> 01:07:35,000
So anyway.

152
01:07:35,000 --> 01:07:41,000
So and of course, if instead of mud, that was a molten lava pit, and people's hands

153
01:07:41,000 --> 01:07:45,000
are tied to the rope so you can't just drop the rope and run away, then people would be

154
01:07:45,000 --> 01:07:50,000
willing to put a lot more resources into hiring people for their team because it's a lot worse

155
01:07:50,000 --> 01:07:54,000
to be pulled into molten lava than to just be pulled into some mud, right?

156
01:07:54,000 --> 01:07:59,000
So and again, similarly, if there's a bigger prize handed out to whoever commits to the

157
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:03,000
government to get on their side, then naturally, people are going to put more resources in

158
01:08:03,000 --> 01:08:07,000
to try and lobby the government to get on that side.

159
01:08:07,000 --> 01:08:08,000
All right.

160
01:08:08,000 --> 01:08:09,000
So this all makes sense.

161
01:08:09,000 --> 01:08:10,000
Familiar?

162
01:08:10,000 --> 01:08:11,000
Maybe.

163
01:08:11,000 --> 01:08:12,000
Or edified, anyway.

164
01:08:12,000 --> 01:08:19,000
You might be delighted at the intellectual understanding and yet horrified at the strategic

165
01:08:19,000 --> 01:08:20,000
situation.

166
01:08:20,000 --> 01:08:22,000
Horrified to know this is going on.

167
01:08:22,000 --> 01:08:23,000
All right.

168
01:08:23,000 --> 01:08:28,000
Now, Gordon Tullock, who did not actually use this phrase when I was speaking, so that

169
01:08:28,000 --> 01:08:29,000
was Ann Krueger.

170
01:08:29,000 --> 01:08:33,000
But anyway, so but Gordon Tullock actually had the idea before her.

171
01:08:33,000 --> 01:08:37,000
So no, no, I don't think there's any evidence that she actually just stole the idea from

172
01:08:37,000 --> 01:08:41,000
me, it's like she just came up with it independently, because Tullock's great article was published

173
01:08:41,000 --> 01:08:43,000
in the Western Economic Journal.

174
01:08:43,000 --> 01:08:45,000
Does that even exist anymore?

175
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:49,000
So, well, the answer is that it was used to name these economic inquiries.

176
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:52,000
Eventually, they realized, wait, Western Economic Journal sounds lame.

177
01:08:52,000 --> 01:08:55,000
So let me call it Economic Inquiry, and that sounds cool.

178
01:08:55,000 --> 01:08:58,000
All right, so just re-branding, but anyway.

179
01:08:58,000 --> 01:09:02,000
So Tullock had this article, and probably, you know, Krueger never read it.

180
01:09:02,000 --> 01:09:05,000
But the deep insight that Tullock had was this.

181
01:09:05,000 --> 01:09:09,000
So you can think about this lobbying or rent-seeking as being an industry, a competitive industry

182
01:09:09,000 --> 01:09:11,000
like any other.

183
01:09:11,000 --> 01:09:16,000
You could invest in putting in more factories into your building, or more or better air

184
01:09:16,000 --> 01:09:19,000
conditioning, or hiring a team of lobbyists, right?

185
01:09:19,000 --> 01:09:24,000
And there's a standard logic about how firms allocate their investment, which is if you've

186
01:09:24,000 --> 01:09:33,000
got one investment earning 5% and the other one earning 7%, what do you do with your resources?

187
01:09:33,000 --> 01:09:35,000
Yes, you reallocate.

188
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:42,000
You reallocate until you've achieved a quality of the rate of return of the marginal dollar spent.

189
01:09:42,000 --> 01:09:44,000
All right, and that is Tullock's story.

190
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:47,000
So he says, lobbying is earning 10%, everything else is earning 5%.

191
01:09:47,000 --> 01:09:53,000
Then resources will flow into the lobbying industry until you reach the usual break-even point.

192
01:09:53,000 --> 01:09:59,000
And everyone remembers how when we say zero profits in economics, why is it that this

193
01:09:59,000 --> 01:10:03,000
doesn't mean that it's stupid to start a business if you're earning zero profits?

194
01:10:03,000 --> 01:10:04,000
Yes?

195
01:10:04,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Because economic profits include your opportunity cost.

196
01:10:06,000 --> 01:10:07,000
Yes, yes.

197
01:10:07,000 --> 01:10:08,000
Everyone remember this?

198
01:10:08,000 --> 01:10:10,000
Economic profits are not the same as accounting profits.

199
01:10:10,000 --> 01:10:11,000
Easily forgotten.

200
01:10:11,000 --> 01:10:15,000
It's also one of the things that probably leads economists to think, or leads business

201
01:10:15,000 --> 01:10:17,000
people to think economists are idiots.

202
01:10:17,000 --> 01:10:19,000
Because we say profits are driven to zero.

203
01:10:19,000 --> 01:10:20,000
It's like, we need profits that are driven to zero.

204
01:10:20,000 --> 01:10:22,000
Why would anyone do anything if profits are zero?

205
01:10:22,000 --> 01:10:27,000
Well, we're including the opportunity cost of interest as that.

206
01:10:27,000 --> 01:10:28,000
Yes.

207
01:10:28,000 --> 01:10:29,000
All right.

208
01:10:29,000 --> 01:10:34,000
So anyway, so you expect new entry as long as you're earning any kind of special profit

209
01:10:34,000 --> 01:10:35,000
in lobbying.

210
01:10:35,000 --> 01:10:40,000
And this is actually very similar to this mixed strategy reasoning, which is if there's

211
01:10:40,000 --> 01:10:45,000
any one thing that's, any one kind of investment that's doing better than others, then people

212
01:10:45,000 --> 01:10:49,000
switch over to the one that's doing better or the one that's doing worse until we reestablish

213
01:10:49,000 --> 01:10:51,000
a quality payoffs.

214
01:10:51,000 --> 01:10:53,000
All right.

215
01:10:53,000 --> 01:11:00,000
Now, so if firms keep going into this tug of war, or someone's going to arms race, well

216
01:11:00,000 --> 01:11:06,000
once economic profits are zero, the same as net profits are zero, and if this is what

217
01:11:06,000 --> 01:11:13,000
government is giving away, so this is the freebie that government is giving away, then

218
01:11:13,000 --> 01:11:18,000
how much, what does lobbying have to equal in order to wipe out those profits?

219
01:11:18,000 --> 01:11:20,000
The size of that rectangle.

220
01:11:20,000 --> 01:11:22,000
Yes, it's got to be the whole rectangle.

221
01:11:22,000 --> 01:11:26,000
If government is giving away a million dollar piece of paper, then you'd expect that people

222
01:11:26,000 --> 01:11:30,000
keep fighting over it until on average they're breaking even.

223
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:34,000
And the way you break even is if the total expense that people are putting in equals

224
01:11:34,000 --> 01:11:35,000
the total value.

225
01:11:35,000 --> 01:11:36,000
All right.

226
01:11:36,000 --> 01:11:43,000
So then this thing, which until now almost everyone's been calling producer surplus,

227
01:11:43,000 --> 01:11:48,000
gets transformed into yet another kind of inefficiency.

228
01:11:48,000 --> 01:11:54,000
It's turned into another dreaded inefficient geometric figure, and this one is called

229
01:11:54,000 --> 01:11:59,000
a tallic rectangle.

230
01:11:59,000 --> 01:12:06,000
A tallic rectangle looks exactly like a monopoly profit rectangle, but it's not monopoly profit.

231
01:12:06,000 --> 01:12:08,000
It's waste.

232
01:12:08,000 --> 01:12:10,000
It is waste.

233
01:12:10,000 --> 01:12:11,000
All right.

234
01:12:11,000 --> 01:12:19,000
And so we get this gruesome story of destruction from three different forms of inefficiency.

235
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:25,000
We've got the allocated inefficiency caused by price being greater than marginal cost.

236
01:12:25,000 --> 01:12:30,000
We've got the productive inefficiency caused by having inefficiently high cost producers

237
01:12:30,000 --> 01:12:31,000
produce.

238
01:12:31,000 --> 01:12:37,000
And then we got the rent-seeking or lobbying inefficiency caused by competition to get

239
01:12:37,000 --> 01:12:39,000
monopoly privilege.

240
01:12:39,000 --> 01:12:40,000
All right.

241
01:12:40,000 --> 01:12:44,000
So notice we start off with a situation where all of that is social surplus, and we end

242
01:12:44,000 --> 01:12:47,000
up with that little tiny residual triangle.

243
01:12:47,000 --> 01:12:48,000
Yes.

244
01:12:48,000 --> 01:12:58,000
So it seems the lobbying inefficiency is also kind of like a negative inefficiency because

245
01:12:58,000 --> 01:13:00,000
it's transferred to somebody else.

246
01:13:00,000 --> 01:13:01,000
Well, yeah.

247
01:13:01,000 --> 01:13:06,000
So of course, we could just say it's all inefficiency and not draw the distinction, but conventionally

248
01:13:06,000 --> 01:13:11,000
we just go and distinguish these three different sources because we're seeing, so this inefficiency,

249
01:13:11,000 --> 01:13:14,000
this is not caused by price being greater than marginal cost.

250
01:13:14,000 --> 01:13:15,000
Right?

251
01:13:15,000 --> 01:13:18,000
That's inefficiency, price being greater than marginal cost.

252
01:13:18,000 --> 01:13:20,000
This is caused by a different route.

253
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:24,000
It's caused by people competing to affect the distribution of resources, burning resources

254
01:13:24,000 --> 01:13:25,000
to do that.

255
01:13:25,000 --> 01:13:27,000
Whereas here you're not burning resources to do it.

256
01:13:27,000 --> 01:13:29,000
You just raise the price.

257
01:13:29,000 --> 01:13:36,000
There's no actual expenditure of resources to go and achieve that waste.

258
01:13:36,000 --> 01:13:37,000
Okay?

259
01:13:37,000 --> 01:13:41,000
And if that ever happens, that is called full reticipation.

260
01:13:41,000 --> 01:13:47,000
Full reticipation when there is so much fighting caused by the effort to control the distribution

261
01:13:47,000 --> 01:13:51,000
of resources that it winds up burning up the whole thing.

262
01:13:51,000 --> 01:13:55,000
Now notice, suppose there's 10 firms that all compete.

263
01:13:55,000 --> 01:14:00,000
So is it every individual firm needs to spend that much or is it all the firms put together

264
01:14:00,000 --> 01:14:06,000
need to spend that much to reach the zero profit condition?

265
01:14:06,000 --> 01:14:08,000
All firms put together.

266
01:14:08,000 --> 01:14:09,000
Right?

267
01:14:09,000 --> 01:14:15,000
Because if every firm spent that, then actually you'd be spending the total value of the gain

268
01:14:15,000 --> 01:14:19,000
for a 10% chance of getting it, which would be stupid.

269
01:14:19,000 --> 01:14:20,000
Right?

270
01:14:20,000 --> 01:14:24,000
So what this means is that the winner generally still is going to have a net profit, but then

271
01:14:24,000 --> 01:14:27,000
there's going to be a bunch of losers that would have been better off if they hadn't

272
01:14:27,000 --> 01:14:28,000
even tried.

273
01:14:28,000 --> 01:14:34,000
And then the total social loss to all the lobbying is that triangle, or rather rectangle.

274
01:14:34,000 --> 01:14:35,000
All right.

275
01:14:35,000 --> 01:14:41,000
Now, so let's go a couple minutes over the break and then you'll still get your full

