85
01:23:25,680 --> 01:23:28,160
So the exam.

86
01:23:28,160 --> 01:23:29,800
First of all, general content.

87
01:23:29,800 --> 01:23:35,520
So something like 50% from lecture, 30% from homework,

88
01:23:35,520 --> 01:23:38,960
about 20% for readings.

89
01:23:38,960 --> 01:23:40,960
Remember how there are readings for this class?

90
01:23:40,960 --> 01:23:43,240
This is where we see whether you've in fact read

91
01:23:43,240 --> 01:23:44,960
and understood them.

92
01:23:44,960 --> 01:23:46,920
Very important point.

93
01:23:46,920 --> 01:23:51,680
The exam is totally open book and note.

94
01:23:51,680 --> 01:23:55,640
So you are free to bring any non-electronic materials

95
01:23:55,640 --> 01:23:56,520
you want.

96
01:23:56,520 --> 01:24:00,400
However, you may not use an electronic device of any kind,

97
01:24:00,400 --> 01:24:04,080
even a calculator, because of the internet and the possibility

98
01:24:04,080 --> 01:24:07,280
that someone is actually feeding you the answers.

99
01:24:07,280 --> 01:24:10,080
So I realize that is pretty unlikely.

100
01:24:10,080 --> 01:24:12,480
But nevertheless, it's just too hard.

101
01:24:12,480 --> 01:24:13,880
When I first started teaching this,

102
01:24:13,880 --> 01:24:15,480
it was just a theoretical possibility.

103
01:24:15,480 --> 01:24:16,920
Now someone really could.

104
01:24:16,920 --> 01:24:19,040
You could photograph the exam.

105
01:24:19,040 --> 01:24:21,040
Someone could do it and then photograph it back.

106
01:24:21,040 --> 01:24:23,040
And you could be transcribing it pretty quickly.

107
01:24:23,040 --> 01:24:25,040
But if you were stuck on one or two questions,

108
01:24:25,040 --> 01:24:27,000
it would be really easy.

109
01:24:27,000 --> 01:24:30,200
You just move on and wait for your accomplice

110
01:24:30,200 --> 01:24:33,320
to send you the correct answer.

111
01:24:33,320 --> 01:24:35,040
So anyway, it's fully open book and note.

112
01:24:35,040 --> 01:24:37,320
But this means that you are free to print off

113
01:24:37,320 --> 01:24:39,720
your entire hard drive if you want.

114
01:24:39,720 --> 01:24:42,240
You can bring a dictionary if you want.

115
01:24:42,240 --> 01:24:44,600
So all this is perfectly fine.

116
01:24:44,600 --> 01:24:47,000
So remember, you're only going to have 100 minutes

117
01:24:47,000 --> 01:24:49,920
to do the exam.

118
01:24:49,920 --> 01:24:53,080
So this is plenty of time for you

119
01:24:53,080 --> 01:24:58,080
to double check something that you have forgotten in a panic.

120
01:24:58,080 --> 01:25:00,520
It's not enough time for you to learn the material,

121
01:25:00,520 --> 01:25:03,520
not enough time for you to read the papers.

122
01:25:03,520 --> 01:25:04,400
That's just my guess.

123
01:25:04,400 --> 01:25:06,400
Maybe you want to write one.

124
01:25:06,400 --> 01:25:07,840
That's fine.

125
01:25:07,840 --> 01:25:09,000
Let's see.

126
01:25:09,000 --> 01:25:10,840
Now, other advice.

127
01:25:10,840 --> 01:25:12,640
So what's the best way to study?

128
01:25:12,640 --> 01:25:16,600
So there are now something like 17 old midterms

129
01:25:16,600 --> 01:25:18,720
on the class web page.

130
01:25:18,720 --> 01:25:21,400
So the best thing to do would be to take those old midterms

131
01:25:21,400 --> 01:25:23,120
under time pressure.

132
01:25:23,120 --> 01:25:26,600
That's the best way to practice is by doing.

133
01:25:26,600 --> 01:25:28,680
So I have thought this for a long time.

134
01:25:28,680 --> 01:25:30,680
But then I started reading Educational Psychology

135
01:25:30,680 --> 01:25:32,880
and found out I was correct, namely

136
01:25:32,880 --> 01:25:34,960
that merely rereading notes to yourself

137
01:25:34,960 --> 01:25:37,160
does not actually teach you very much.

138
01:25:37,160 --> 01:25:39,720
You need to actually do problems, working problems.

139
01:25:39,720 --> 01:25:42,120
Because if you're reading notes, it's very easy to say, yes, yes,

140
01:25:42,120 --> 01:25:42,920
of course, I understand.

141
01:25:42,920 --> 01:25:44,920
Of course, it's also obvious, of course.

142
01:25:44,920 --> 01:25:46,680
And then you're given a problem asking

143
01:25:46,680 --> 01:25:47,880
you to apply it to the notes.

144
01:25:47,880 --> 01:25:49,960
And that's where many people will flounder and say,

145
01:25:49,960 --> 01:25:52,080
realize, wow, I don't think I know where to start.

146
01:25:52,080 --> 01:25:54,880
So working problems is the best approach.

147
01:25:54,880 --> 01:25:56,800
And the time pressure is important, too.

148
01:25:56,800 --> 01:25:58,880
The fact that you can work problems successfully

149
01:25:58,880 --> 01:26:01,240
given unlimited time doesn't mean you can actually

150
01:26:01,240 --> 01:26:02,880
do it under test pressure.

151
01:26:02,880 --> 01:26:06,360
So that's very good to do as well.

152
01:26:06,360 --> 01:26:06,860
Let's see.

153
01:26:06,860 --> 01:26:08,640
More general principles.

154
01:26:08,640 --> 01:26:10,520
This is one that goes beyond this class,

155
01:26:10,520 --> 01:26:12,160
but it's a great principle.

156
01:26:12,160 --> 01:26:19,880
And it's called answer the question.

157
01:26:19,880 --> 01:26:22,200
This might seem really obvious, especially given

158
01:26:22,200 --> 01:26:23,480
how far you've gotten.

159
01:26:23,480 --> 01:26:25,960
But I was just grading some homeworks today

160
01:26:25,960 --> 01:26:27,080
from another class.

161
01:26:27,080 --> 01:26:31,000
And, wow, most of them just didn't answer the question.

162
01:26:31,000 --> 01:26:34,640
So for example, if there is a question that

163
01:26:34,640 --> 01:26:38,040
says give an original example of a coordination game

164
01:26:38,040 --> 01:26:41,220
and discuss how you could move from a worse

165
01:26:41,220 --> 01:26:43,480
equilibrium to a better one, should you

166
01:26:43,480 --> 01:26:45,600
begin by saying a coordination game

167
01:26:45,600 --> 01:26:48,000
is a game with the following properties, blah, blah, blah,

168
01:26:48,000 --> 01:26:49,280
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah?

169
01:26:49,280 --> 01:26:49,800
No.

170
01:26:49,800 --> 01:26:50,960
No.

171
01:26:50,960 --> 01:26:52,720
Because the question did not ask you

172
01:26:52,720 --> 01:26:54,480
to explain what a coordination game is.

173
01:26:54,480 --> 01:26:56,320
It asked you for an example.

174
01:26:56,320 --> 01:27:00,320
So if the question says give a novel example of a coordination

175
01:27:00,320 --> 01:27:02,480
game, what would be a great first sentence

176
01:27:02,480 --> 01:27:04,680
for that question, for your response?

177
01:27:04,680 --> 01:27:06,760
A novel example of a coordination game is?

178
01:27:06,760 --> 01:27:07,400
Yes.

179
01:27:07,400 --> 01:27:12,120
That's a wonderful, wonderful first sentence for your answer.

180
01:27:12,120 --> 01:27:13,540
I know you're under test pressure.

181
01:27:13,540 --> 01:27:15,120
You don't need to be eloquent.

182
01:27:15,120 --> 01:27:19,440
But if you just get to the point immediately, this is great.

183
01:27:19,440 --> 01:27:23,680
Now, I try to read every word students write.

184
01:27:23,680 --> 01:27:27,320
But if you go and spend half your time not answering

185
01:27:27,320 --> 01:27:29,360
the question, what are the odds that your answers

186
01:27:29,360 --> 01:27:31,320
are going to be competitive with those of people

187
01:27:31,320 --> 01:27:32,840
who got to the point?

188
01:27:32,840 --> 01:27:33,920
It's just hard.

189
01:27:33,920 --> 01:27:37,840
If you spend most of your energy giving me back story,

190
01:27:37,840 --> 01:27:41,760
you're like, economics is a subject which we can.

191
01:27:41,760 --> 01:27:44,400
With the sped scholastics.

192
01:27:44,400 --> 01:27:48,040
And then, page after page, and then finally, oh, yeah,

193
01:27:48,040 --> 01:27:49,440
what was the question again?

194
01:27:49,440 --> 01:27:51,800
So don't do that.

195
01:27:51,800 --> 01:27:56,760
And I'll even add in, how about answer the literal question?

196
01:27:56,760 --> 01:27:59,880
The exact words, even better.

197
01:27:59,880 --> 01:28:02,680
So once in a long while, someone answers a question

198
01:28:02,680 --> 01:28:04,180
even more literally than I intended,

199
01:28:04,180 --> 01:28:06,280
and I always give them credit.

200
01:28:06,280 --> 01:28:08,720
Because I say, wow, they were even more literal

201
01:28:08,720 --> 01:28:10,480
than I had expected.

202
01:28:10,480 --> 01:28:12,680
But usually, the problem is people

203
01:28:12,680 --> 01:28:14,040
don't answer a literal question.

204
01:28:14,040 --> 01:28:17,040
Instead, they try to say, well, what

205
01:28:17,040 --> 01:28:19,240
is the general spirit of this question?

206
01:28:19,240 --> 01:28:20,740
And then they talk about the spirit.

207
01:28:20,740 --> 01:28:22,920
And again, not good.

208
01:28:22,920 --> 01:28:25,640
Because if someone answers the spirit of a question

209
01:28:25,640 --> 01:28:27,880
while they've not answered the question,

210
01:28:27,880 --> 01:28:30,640
this leaves me wondering, did the person

211
01:28:30,640 --> 01:28:34,040
know the answer to the actual literal question?

212
01:28:34,040 --> 01:28:37,960
So I do try to figure out whether you do or not.

213
01:28:37,960 --> 01:28:43,080
But again, the easiest thing is just to remove all doubt.

214
01:28:43,080 --> 01:28:45,960
Don't give me any room to wonder whether you

215
01:28:45,960 --> 01:28:48,080
know, because you immediately did answer

216
01:28:48,080 --> 01:28:49,320
an exactly literal question.

217
01:28:49,320 --> 01:28:52,880
So if it says, discuss the reason for x

218
01:28:52,880 --> 01:28:55,560
and give two examples.

219
01:28:55,560 --> 01:28:57,080
So here's the reason for this.

220
01:28:57,080 --> 01:28:58,080
Here's example one.

221
01:28:58,080 --> 01:28:59,320
Here's example two.

222
01:28:59,320 --> 01:29:00,960
Literally answered.

223
01:29:00,960 --> 01:29:01,520
Done.

224
01:29:01,520 --> 01:29:02,760
Great.

225
01:29:02,760 --> 01:29:03,240
OK.

226
01:29:03,240 --> 01:29:05,320
So that's very useful.

227
01:29:05,320 --> 01:29:06,760
And then there's one other thing.

228
01:29:06,760 --> 01:29:11,200
This is a intellectual failing that people generally

229
01:29:11,200 --> 01:29:12,440
acquire in economics.

230
01:29:12,440 --> 01:29:15,040
Almost no one has it until they come to economics.

231
01:29:15,040 --> 01:29:16,720
And then many people never lose it.

232
01:29:16,720 --> 01:29:18,160
So it's bad.

233
01:29:18,160 --> 01:29:20,320
So again, I was just raising hallmarks

234
01:29:20,320 --> 01:29:22,640
where there's some experienced economists, more advanced

235
01:29:22,640 --> 01:29:26,720
than you, who still seem to not understand

236
01:29:26,720 --> 01:29:32,520
questions that involve the real world.

237
01:29:32,520 --> 01:29:35,440
So there's a question that says, give a real world

238
01:29:35,440 --> 01:29:38,960
example of rent seeking.

239
01:29:38,960 --> 01:29:40,400
That's kind of easy, but anyway.

240
01:29:40,400 --> 01:29:43,840
So would a good answer then be saying,

241
01:29:43,840 --> 01:29:47,840
assume that there is a widget company which produces

242
01:29:47,840 --> 01:29:50,280
and it is seeking a monopoly privilege for widget

243
01:29:50,280 --> 01:29:51,240
production.

244
01:29:51,240 --> 01:29:55,120
Is that a good example of a real world example of rent seeking?

245
01:29:55,120 --> 01:29:56,880
No.

246
01:29:56,880 --> 01:29:58,240
Exactly.

247
01:29:58,240 --> 01:30:02,240
So if you just make up something, or you invent numbers,

248
01:30:02,240 --> 01:30:05,360
or you start saying, talk about the real world,

249
01:30:05,360 --> 01:30:09,560
and then you say, well, assume zero transactions costs.

250
01:30:09,560 --> 01:30:11,360
It's very hard to give people a rich credit

251
01:30:11,360 --> 01:30:14,960
for answers like this, because again, what

252
01:30:14,960 --> 01:30:18,560
could be a good real world example of rent seeking?

253
01:30:18,560 --> 01:30:22,440
Just remember, just a few minutes ago before the break.

254
01:30:22,440 --> 01:30:23,600
Comcast.

255
01:30:23,600 --> 01:30:24,280
Comcast.

256
01:30:24,280 --> 01:30:24,760
Yes.

257
01:30:24,760 --> 01:30:25,400
All right, yes.

258
01:30:25,400 --> 01:30:26,880
So Comcast, trying to do that.

259
01:30:26,880 --> 01:30:30,120
Or the Chinese examination system.

260
01:30:30,120 --> 01:30:33,200
Or just anything like that.

261
01:30:33,200 --> 01:30:36,880
Of course, again, in general, if you give an original example,

262
01:30:36,880 --> 01:30:38,080
that's a bit more convincing.

263
01:30:38,080 --> 01:30:42,320
Usually, I will actually say, give an original example.

264
01:30:42,320 --> 01:30:44,480
Because if you just spit back my own example to me,

265
01:30:44,480 --> 01:30:46,560
then I do have to wonder, does the person actually

266
01:30:46,560 --> 01:30:48,000
know how to apply the concept?

267
01:30:48,000 --> 01:30:50,520
Or are they simply using what I taught them?

268
01:30:50,520 --> 01:30:53,760
Now, on the other hand, this is one kind of phrasing.

269
01:30:53,760 --> 01:30:56,560
There's another kind of phrasing that says,

270
01:30:56,560 --> 01:31:04,160
in a Bertrand model, then would it

271
01:31:04,160 --> 01:31:05,880
be relevant to say that real firms don't

272
01:31:05,880 --> 01:31:07,840
do marginal cost pricing?

273
01:31:07,840 --> 01:31:08,560
No.

274
01:31:08,560 --> 01:31:11,160
So if it mentions a model, talk about what

275
01:31:11,160 --> 01:31:12,600
happens in the model.

276
01:31:12,600 --> 01:31:15,160
Talks about the real world, talk about the real world.

277
01:31:18,040 --> 01:31:22,440
And yeah, so normally, they'll say,

278
01:31:22,440 --> 01:31:25,040
as a general rule, if it doesn't say,

279
01:31:25,040 --> 01:31:27,160
talk about this particular model,

280
01:31:27,160 --> 01:31:29,120
or a particular assumption in a model,

281
01:31:29,120 --> 01:31:31,840
then the question is actually about the real world.

282
01:31:31,840 --> 01:31:34,240
On the other hand, if I go and give you a payoff matrix

283
01:31:34,240 --> 01:31:36,440
like this, then of course, you're

284
01:31:36,440 --> 01:31:39,160
supposed to work with a payoff matrix.

285
01:31:39,160 --> 01:31:42,240
So in general, on the true-false explained questions,

286
01:31:42,240 --> 01:31:43,720
you're more likely to have models,

287
01:31:43,720 --> 01:31:45,800
and on the essays, you're more likely to be

288
01:31:45,800 --> 01:31:46,680
about the real world.

289
01:31:46,680 --> 01:31:49,120
But that's not a hard and fast rule.

290
01:31:49,120 --> 01:31:51,200
It could go either way.

291
01:31:51,200 --> 01:31:55,200
Let's see, so I think those are new things now.

292
01:31:55,200 --> 01:31:59,400
And again, like I said, so if you are in any way troubled,

293
01:31:59,400 --> 01:32:02,960
there are hundreds and hundreds of old exam questions

294
01:32:02,960 --> 01:32:04,880
that you can access.

295
01:32:04,880 --> 01:32:06,720
Once in a long while, I recycle one,

296
01:32:06,720 --> 01:32:09,520
although normally, I change a few words.

297
01:32:09,520 --> 01:32:12,200
Sometimes changing the words changes the answer.

298
01:32:12,200 --> 01:32:14,600
Other times, it does not change the answer.

299
01:32:14,600 --> 01:32:17,200
You are totally entitled to print off every old exam

300
01:32:17,200 --> 01:32:20,400
and then bring it to the exam and desperately

301
01:32:20,400 --> 01:32:21,520
try to fumble through them.

302
01:32:21,520 --> 01:32:25,560
But once in a while, that works.

303
01:32:25,560 --> 01:32:28,320
Once many years ago, I had two undergraduates

304
01:32:28,320 --> 01:32:30,160
who I thought had cheated off of each other.

305
01:32:30,160 --> 01:32:33,760
And then they pointed out that I had, in fact, directly

306
01:32:33,760 --> 01:32:35,280
recycled an old exam question.

307
01:32:35,280 --> 01:32:37,360
And they had both simply copied it,

308
01:32:37,360 --> 01:32:38,800
which is totally within the rules.

309
01:32:38,800 --> 01:32:42,640
And I thought, oh, so sorry to have accused you of something.

310
01:32:42,640 --> 01:32:45,360
I was puzzled as to how they were opposite ends of the room.

311
01:32:45,360 --> 01:32:46,480
So I was a little puzzled.

312
01:32:46,480 --> 01:32:48,520
I thought a cheating had occurred.

313
01:32:48,520 --> 01:32:50,840
But what are the odds that they would both

314
01:32:50,840 --> 01:32:52,240
use exactly these sentences?

315
01:32:57,960 --> 01:33:00,640
At the time, I refrained from directly accusing them

316
01:33:00,640 --> 01:33:03,480
and just said, can you explain this?

317
01:33:03,480 --> 01:33:07,040
Afterwards, I are even more cautious in my accusations.

318
01:33:07,040 --> 01:33:10,400
So I say, look, I'm just quickly explaining.

319
01:33:10,400 --> 01:33:14,080
I'm just trying to be very gentle because I realized,

320
01:33:14,080 --> 01:33:16,560
wow, even when I thought it was a clear-cut case of cheating,

321
01:33:16,560 --> 01:33:18,880
I was wrong.

322
01:33:18,880 --> 01:33:21,760
So that's my general thing to say.

323
01:33:21,760 --> 01:33:24,000
Oh, yeah, the other thing is that when I go and select

324
01:33:24,000 --> 01:33:27,120
questions, I am playing mixed strategy and Nash equilibrium.

325
01:33:27,120 --> 01:33:28,120
So I am trying.

326
01:33:28,120 --> 01:33:28,600
I am not.

327
01:33:28,600 --> 01:33:29,720
There is no predictability.

328
01:33:29,720 --> 01:33:31,300
Or I am trying to avoid predictability

329
01:33:31,300 --> 01:33:33,200
in what I will ask you about.

330
01:33:33,200 --> 01:33:35,400
I am trying to be unpredictable to give you incentives

331
01:33:35,400 --> 01:33:38,000
to learn all the material.

332
01:33:38,000 --> 01:33:40,320
Perhaps I am not as good at randomizing as I think.

333
01:33:40,320 --> 01:33:42,240
And you can discern a pattern in old exams.

334
01:33:42,240 --> 01:33:46,080
And then you can exploit this to not learn the subject

335
01:33:46,080 --> 01:33:47,920
that you were trying to become a doctor of.

336
01:33:47,920 --> 01:33:53,520
And that's up to you.

337
01:33:53,520 --> 01:33:56,720
All right, so other purely bureaucratic questions.

338
01:33:56,720 --> 01:33:57,320
Yes, please.

339
01:33:57,320 --> 01:33:58,400
I just want to make sure.

340
01:33:58,400 --> 01:33:59,920
So you said no calculators.

341
01:33:59,920 --> 01:34:02,440
Correct, because a calculator now can have internet access.

342
01:34:02,440 --> 01:34:02,940
Oh.

343
01:34:02,940 --> 01:34:04,480
And then a normal scientific?

344
01:34:04,480 --> 01:34:04,980
Yes.

345
01:34:04,980 --> 01:34:06,440
And then a master, will you assume?

346
01:34:06,440 --> 01:34:07,880
So some scientific ones have them.

347
01:34:07,880 --> 01:34:09,960
And again, it's too hard to verify which ones do

348
01:34:09,960 --> 01:34:11,720
and don't at this point.

349
01:34:11,720 --> 01:34:14,320
The world is just too confusing now.

350
01:34:14,320 --> 01:34:15,240
So yeah.

351
01:34:15,240 --> 01:34:18,040
So this does mean that you weren't expected

352
01:34:18,040 --> 01:34:19,560
to actually use a calculator.

